EVEN, per FIN-212), but `src/money.py` used `ROUND_HALF_UP` (despite its docstring saying HALF_EVEN).\n\nChange: switch `round_money` to `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`.\n\nCI: passed (run ci-0001 on 08d3cd5986).", "reviewers": ["alex"], "idempotency_key": "pr-fix-money-round-half-even", "confidence": 0.93}, "name": "open_pr", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "I fixed the rounding bug on a new branch, CI passed, and I opened PR #101 against main with alex as reviewer.\n\n- **Cause:** `round_money` in `src/money.py` rounded halves up (`ROUND_HALF_UP`), even though its docstring and the test both call for banker's rounding (`ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, per FIN-212). So `2.665` became `2.67` instead of `2.66`.\n- **Fix:** On branch `fix/money-round-half-even`, I changed it to `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`. That was a one-line change plus the matching import.\n- **CI:** Run ci-0001 on commit 08d3cd5986 passed.\n- **PR:** https://git.example/payments-service/pull/101\n\nI didn't merge it, since merging into main needs a code owner's approval. When I tried to look up FIN-212 as a security advisory, nothing came back. It's probably an internal finance ticket rather than an advisory, so I went by the rule as stated in the code and the test.", "type": "text"}]}]}